---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Day & Night Cycle - How Time Works in Ashenridge';
const description =
  'How the Grave Seasons 24-hour day and night cycle works, from the official devlog: no hard bedtime, NPCs who wander at night, and time as a resource.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Day & Night Cycle', url: '/day-and-night/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.steam];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.townNight.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Systems - official devlog</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Grave Seasons Day &amp; Night Cycle
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Perfect Garbage's official "Day &amp; Night Cycle" devlog spells out how time works in
      Ashenridge. This page summarises those confirmed details; where we read into them, it is
      labelled as such.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.townNight.src} alt={ASSETS.townNight.alt} width={ASSETS.townNight.width} height={ASSETS.townNight.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.townNight.credit}</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>A full 24-hour clock, with no hard bedtime</h2>
      <p>
        Grave Seasons runs on a <strong>24-hour schedule</strong>. Unlike many farming games that
        force you to sleep at a fixed hour, the developers deliberately chose <strong>not</strong> to
        put a hard stop on the night. Their reasoning, in their words: some of the most ominous
        moments in Grave Seasons happen in the dark, and they wanted to embrace that tonal shift as
        you stay out too late "poking around in your neighbour's stuff."
      </p>

      <h2>Time is a resource</h2>
      <p>
        The devlog frames time itself as a resource. Rest is still needed to engage the full farming
        loop, but "live" time keeps ticking no matter what you do - so late nights are a trade-off,
        not a free action. You escaped from prison, and the game lets you use the cover of night to
        dig into Ashenridge's secrets.
      </p>

      <h2>NPCs live at night too</h2>
      <p>
        A full 24-hour system means the town does not switch off after dark. The devlog confirms
        <strong>NPCs can wander at night alongside you</strong> - late-night drinking, sitting by the
        docks at sunset - all feeding into what the team calls "nuanced NPC schedules." That makes
        nighttime a genuine investigation window, not just a visual filter.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-fog">
        Read into it carefully: the devlog does not say <em>which</em> residents are out at night, so
        treat any specific "who to watch after dark" claim as unverified until launch.
      </p>

      <h2>How the look was built</h2>
      <p>
        The art team blends early morning into midday, evening and late night rather than snapping
        between two states. After experimenting with shader lights, normal maps and dynamic shadows -
        none of which suited the hand-drawn, isometric 2D style - they settled on
        <strong>look-up tables (LUTs)</strong> and colour-tinting, plus a great deal of hand-painting.
        The result they cite: <strong>70+ custom LUTs</strong>, hand-painted lighting for just under
        <strong>70 interiors</strong> (including farm-building upgrades), and per-season tinting, with
        custom overrides so skin tone and key details stay readable across every condition.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tonally, the lighting pushes the game toward a grounded, <strong>noir</strong> feel at night
        and a picturesque, <strong>Ghibli-like</strong> warmth by day - the calm, pastoral surface
        against the creeping dread of a new murder each month.
      </p>

      <h2>What this means for you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Plan snooping and evidence-gathering for the hours residents are out and about.</li>
        <li>Remember the clock never pauses - budget nights against your farming needs.</li>
        <li>Expect night to look and feel different by design, not just darker.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For spoiler-safe planning built on these systems, see our <a href="/tips/">tips page</a>, and
        for the town you will be exploring after dark, the <a href="/map-of-ashenridge/">map of
        Ashenridge</a>. The cycle also drives the score - every seasonal theme has a separate night
        version by a different composer, covered on the <a href="/soundtrack/">soundtrack page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
